			<div class="content" id="post-content-1632682">
									

<p>Thank you. Most of the issues here are now addressed. As for those that are not, I will at least say why.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;Affected by gravity, yet registers no weight.&quot; That's what gravity is.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Weight is a measurement of an object's mass as affected by gravity. It is pulled downward by gravity, but does not register any weight upon scales/other instruments. It does not return any tactile sensation when touched. These give something of an idea of its properties.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;Hostilities began, containment was breached.&quot; Poor tone, bad comma.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not sure how to rephrase this one.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The recovery log is one paragraph of recovery log followed by two unrelated to its recovery: the second paragraph is about its inferred or deduced behavior prior to containment, while the third is about the Foundation's exploratory interactions with it after containment.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The recovery log deals with more than simply the recovery, yes, it is the Foundation's initial information they were able to ascertain about it. I felt it applicable. I may rework this later though, I do feel you have a point.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Incident Log is not about a single incident, but instead comprises over 19 months of behavior including at least four or five unique events worthy of being called &quot;incidents.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I was initially going to format this as a table, but it felt wrong to me. Rather than being a group of isolated incidents, all of the incidents are connected as a prolonged attempt at escape/recovery.</p>
<p>The similarity to 1322 is one that I had noted and worried about, but knowing the actual story and background to it I know that it's a very different set of circumstances, so I was willing to move forward with it. Agree that 1322 is better executed though, that's one of the best on the site in my opinion.</p>
<p>That all said - I am impressed at your style of analysis and feedback. Keep it up with others, this was good work and very helpful.</p>

							</div>

								
